From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: az0x <dev@localhost>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 12:43:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] pinctrl: rockchip: accept and ignore slew-rate instead of
 failing

No Rockchip SoC implements slew rate in this driver - the string does not
appear in it at all - so rockchip_pinconf_set() falls through to the default
case and returns -ENOTSUPP for any pin config that asks for it.

That is worse than it sounds. pinctrl_commit_state() treats one rejected
parameter as failure of the entire state and reverts it, so a group that also
sets a mux loses the mux too. On inMusic AZ0x boards the vendor device tree
puts slew-rate on the I2S pin configs, and the result is:

  rockchip-pinctrl pinctrl: pin_config_set op failed for pin 184
  rockchip-i2s ff890000.i2s: Error applying setting, reverse things back

leaving "current state: none" in pinctrl-handles. The sound card still
registers and streams, the DMA runs and the codec GPIOs toggle, but the pins
are never switched to the I2S function, so nothing reaches the DAC - a silent
failure that looks like working audio from userspace.

Accept the parameter and ignore it. The bias and drive-strength settings in
the same group then apply, and the mux survives. Ignoring is what would happen
anyway if the property were simply deleted from the device tree; this keeps
the device tree an honest description of the board instead.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [workaround for an unimplemented feature]
---
 drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-rockchip.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-rockchip.c b/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-rockchip.c
index 0c2382480c24..44d66bda4d82 100644
--- a/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/pinctrl/pinctrl-rockchip.c
@@ -2914,6 +2914,18 @@ static int rockchip_pinconf_set(struct pinctrl_dev *pctldev, unsigned int pin,
 			if (rc < 0)
 				return rc;
 			break;
+		case PIN_CONFIG_SLEW_RATE:
+			/*
+			 * Not implemented for any Rockchip SoC, but accept it
+			 * rather than failing: pinctrl_commit_state() reverts
+			 * the *whole* state when one parameter is rejected, so
+			 * returning -ENOTSUPP here throws away the mux and the
+			 * settings that are supported along with it.
+			 */
+			dev_dbg(info->dev,
+				"slew-rate not supported, ignoring for pin %d\n",
+				pin);
+			break;
 		default:
 			return -ENOTSUPP;
 			break;
